<?php
// Host-hardening declaration template (firewall reconcile data). The real
// values are private data: they live in the private config repo and simox's
// bin/fetch-private-data copies them into etc/host-hardening.php as a real file
// (see doc/system/private-config.md). Never edit that file in place — the next
// fetch overwrites it, so the reconcile would keep reading the private values;
// change the private repo instead. This template is intentionally NOT
// the live simox config: the values below are placeholders only, chosen not to
// mirror the real deployment.
//
// Consumed by the framework gen-firewall: it reads etc/host-hardening.php,
// computes the desired ufw rule set for each [prod] host from its
// etc/machines.ini tags, and applies it as root (fail-open: SSH first,
// enable last). The framework owns the mechanism and the built-in tags; the
// real file carries only consumer data. There is no SQL here — data only.
//
//   $zerotierRange = '10.0.x.0/24';
//   $cloudTest     = array('endpoint' => 'http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/',
//                          'timeout' => 3);
//   $tagRules      = array(
//       'web' => array('80/tcp', '443/tcp'),
//   );
//
// $zerotierRange scopes the baseline SSH and the built-in db:<name> allows.
// A concrete CIDR is used verbatim; an 'x' template resolves its private third
// octet from the consumer's private etc/hosts (the range is never hardcoded).
// $cloudTest is the optional runtime metadata probe that gates gate=>'cloud'
// rules. Any HTTP response (even a 404) counts as cloud; no response/timeout
// means not-cloud (fail-safe). null disables gating.
// $tagRules maps a consumer-owned tag to its allow rules. A rule is either
// 'PORT/PROTO' (a public allow) or array('rule' => 'PORT/PROTO',
// 'gate' => 'cloud') (applied only when the cloud test passes).

$zerotierRange = '10.0.x.0/24';

$cloudTest = array(
    'endpoint' => 'http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/',
    'timeout' => 3,
);

$tagRules = array(
    'web' => array('80/tcp', '443/tcp'),
);
